## How Excalidraw cleans up bindings on deletion

The core logic lives in `packages/excalidraw/element/binding.ts`, in the function **`fixBindingsAfterDeletion`** (called from `actionDeleteSelected.tsx` after elements are soft-deleted, and also from `App.tsx` for other deletion flows):

```ts
export const fixBindingsAfterDeletion = (
  sceneElements: readonly ExcalidrawElement[],
  deletedElements: readonly ExcalidrawElement[],
): void => {
  const elements = arrayToMap(sceneElements);
  for (const element of deletedElements) {
    BoundElement.unbindAffected(elements, element, mutateElement);
    BindableElement.unbindAffected(elements, element, mutateElement);
  }
};
```

Note Excalidraw uses **soft deletion** (`isDeleted: true`, elements stay in the array), so cleanup means scrubbing references *to* the deleted elements out of the surviving elements' data, not removing entries from an array.

For every deleted element it runs two symmetric passes, each using small "visitor" helpers (`bindableElementsVisitor` / `boundElementsVisitor`) that know how to walk an element's binding-related fields (`frameId`, `containerId`, `startBinding`/`endBinding` for arrows, `boundElements` for bindable shapes):

1. **`BoundElement.unbindAffected`** — treats the deleted element as a *bound* element (e.g. a deleted arrow or bound text). It walks the bindable elements it pointed to (its container/start/end binding targets) and, for each still-alive one, filters the deleted element's id out of that target's `boundElements` array (via `newBoundElements`, which just filters out matching ids).

2. **`BindableElement.unbindAffected`** — treats the deleted element as a *bindable* element (e.g. a deleted rectangle/container). It walks its `boundElements` (arrows/text bound to it) and, for each still-alive one, nulls out the reference back to it (sets `containerId`, `startBinding`, or `endBinding` to `null`).

Both visitors explicitly skip elements that are themselves already deleted (`if (!bindableElement || bindableElement.isDeleted) return;`), since dangling refs between two deleted elements don't matter and will just disappear together.

This is the mirror image of `BoundElement.rebindAffected` / `BindableElement.rebindAffected`, which are used on undo/redo or restoration to re-establish bindings that were unbound this way — the code comments explicitly note rebind expects elements were previously unbound via these same `unbindAffected` methods.

So in short: on delete, Excalidraw doesn't hunt for dangling refs after the fact — it proactively walks outward from each just-deleted element in both directions (as a bound element and as a bindable element) and surgically strips its id from every live neighbor's binding fields/`boundElements` list.
